<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rule of the Major-Generals</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1655–1657</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rule of the Major-Generals was a period of direct military government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> from August 1655 to January 1657, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>. After a royalist rising, the country was divided into regions, each under a major-general charged with security, collecting a tax on royalists and enforcing moral reform. Deeply unpopular, it ended when Parliament refused to renew the tax.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rule-of-the-major-generals.jpg" alt="Rule of the Major-Generals" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The wub · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rule_of_the_Major-Generals_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
